Congresswoman Marjorie Taylor Green appears

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:16.800
to have had a I don't really want to call
it a come to Jesus moment because it relates

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.800
to the Holocaust.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.500
Now, you might remember that Marjorie Taylor
Greene equated having to wear a mask on the

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.800
floor of the House of Representatives to how
the Jews were treated during the Holocaust.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.699
And she said mandatory vaccinations are like
the Holocaust even there, even though there

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:33.050
are no mandatory vaccinations.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:38.410
Then she doubled down on the analogy, but
later denied ever having made it.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:43.780
But now Marjorie Taylor Green visited the
Holocaust Museum in Washington, D.C., and

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.850
then made a statement that, you know what,
she was wrong.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.340
She apologizes.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:54.379
The Holocaust was really very bad and there's
nothing comparable to it, presumably meaning

00:00:54.379 --> 00:00:58.340
mask wearing is not like the Holocaust and
vaccinations are not like the Holocaust.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.340
Check this out.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.780
This is Marjorie Taylor Green.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.059
Fresh off of the realization that the Holocaust
was really bad.

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:09.240
Speaker 2: I always want to remind everyone
I'm very much a normal person.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:16.770
And I think it's important for me to always
be transparent and honest.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.960
And I just want to tell you all I'm really,
really lucky.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.000
I was blessed.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.159
I am blessed with amazing parents.

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:30.780
